e haven't covered Candidate D much, and Candidate A.\n\nWe need to decide whether to stick with Candidate C, shift to B, or maybe propose another candidate. The panel currently leans toward B (Agent 3 and 2). We need to either agree, challenge, or bring new info.\n\nWe have our own notes: Candidate C can anticipate dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, but sometimes not good at taking criticism, can be unorganized. Also we have note that Candidate C is sometimes not good at taking criticism (feedback orientation) and can be unorganized (reliability). Those are concerns. The discussion hasn't mentioned those yet. Also we have not discussed attention to detail for any candidate. We could bring up that Candidate C's unorganized nature may affect attention to detail, which is crucial. That could weaken C further.\n\nWe could also bring up Candidate D's strengths: responds adequately to unexpected events, concentrates well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously. Concerns: arrogant, weak leadership. Could be a viable alternative if B's communication issues are severe.\n\nWe need to make a focused contribution: maybe we can say that while B has communication issues, C's unorganized nature and poor feedback receptiveness are more serious for safety, and also we have concerns about attention to detail. Therefore we might still support B, but we need more info on B's crew cooperation. Or we could propose that D might be a better fit because of responsibility and problem solving, but his arrogance is a concern.\n\nGiven the instruction to cover all candidates before final recommendation, we should bring up D now, comparing with B and C. That would be a new point but still connected: \"Given the concerns raised about B and C, we should also consider D.\" That is acceptable.\n\nWe need to state our current recommendation. We could shift to B, aligning with others, but also note that D mi